| **How far can a stranger actually re-derive each result?** | **DECIDED, and it publishes the weak rows louder than the strong ones.** This page has always said "every headline reconciles to an artifact", which is true and is not the same as "every headline is re-derivable". Verifiability is not one property but four, and `scripts/verification_coverage.py` now classifies all 237 banked artifacts mechanically (re-synced 2026-10-21 from the coverage map; first written at 176) rather than leaving a reader to piece it together from footnotes: **PRIMARY** (raw predictions stored, gold regenerable, every prediction re-scorable — trust boundary is the generator seed plus the scorer code); **ARITHMETIC** (per-item scores stored, predictions not — the aggregation is checkable, the step from model output to score is not); **AGGREGATE** (summary statistics only); **EXTERNAL** (third-party quote, not ours to verify). **Result: PRIMARY 56 · ARITHMETIC 81 · AGGREGATE 93 · EXTERNAL 7. Only 24% of what we have banked is primary-verifiable (33 · 78 · 58 · 7 and 19% when this row was first written).** **And the number this page cites most is the worst one on that scale.** The +46.5 headline's summary is AGGREGATE and its arms are ARITHMETIC: their model outputs were discarded at generation time, so the step from output to score cannot be re-checked **by anyone, including us**, at any level. It is the only headline here that is neither primary-verifiable nor regenerable. Re-running those arms with predictions stored is the fix; this row first priced it at *about nine CPU-hours*; the Addendum Z protocol computes ~35–57 wall-hours on four cores and quotes 40–60 (CORRECTIONS 2026-09-23 and 2026-10-21) — wrong by ~5× if the old estimate is read as nine wall-hours of a session, by ~20× if read as literal CPU-hours — and the re-run is preregistered as Addendum Z (PENDING row above), launched after its anchor. **One distinction the classifier deliberately refuses to make, stated so it is not mistaken for a defect:** Addendum K's artifact also reads AGGREGATE, because its writer excluded predictions — but every input it consumed is published and its recipe is pinned to a named commit, so a reader regenerates it in seconds. The +46.5 arms are gone for good. Both read AGGREGATE, only one is recoverable, and **ambiguity downgrades** rather than upgrades, because the failure being guarded against is a page implying more checkability than it has | `experiments/verification_coverage.json` | `PYTHONPATH=src python3 scripts/verification_coverage.py` |
